  "analysis": {
    "one_liner": "Tata Capital is the Tata Group's diversified non-bank lender, financing retail, SME, corporate, housing and vehicle loans, and now entering gold loans.",
    "tags": [
      "P/E ~25x",
      "ROE 10.6%",
      "Promoter 85%",
      "Listed <1yr",
      "Results due Oct 27"
    ],
    "whats_happening": "The board meets on 27 October 2026 (about a week away) to approve Q2 FY27 (July-September 2026) results, so that print is the next real data point. The last reported quarter, Q1 FY27 (April-June 2026), showed consolidated PAT of \u20b91,547 crore, up 56% year-on-year, with AUM up 22% to about \u20b92.9 lakh crore. Separately, the company is entering gold loans by acquiring an 88.6% stake in Yogloans, pending RBI approval targeted by end-CY2026.",
    "plain_terms": "This is a lending business - it borrows money and re-lends it at a margin across home loans, vehicle loans, business loans and now gold loans. The tension: the stock trades at a premium-sounding P/E of about 25x and P/B of about 2.9x, but the actual return on equity is a middling 10.6% and return on capital employed just 8.1% - numbers that look more 'average NBFC' than 'premium franchise'. One tracker (MarketsMojo) has flagged the valuation as stretched and cut its rating. Within the book, the motor-finance piece is noticeably weaker than the rest.",
    "quarters": "Q1 FY27 (Apr-Jun 2026): consolidated PAT \u20b91,547 crore, up 56% YoY; AUM \u20b92,90,502 crore, up 22% YoY (up 28% excluding motor finance). Annualised credit cost fell to 1.0% from 1.6% a year earlier - a meaningful chunk of the profit jump came from lower provisioning rather than pure volume growth, so the 56% PAT growth is not purely scale-driven and may not repeat every quarter if credit costs normalise. Consolidated GNPA improved quarter-on-quarter to about 2.45%, but motor finance alone carried gross stage-3 assets of 7.9% and net stage-3 of 5.1% as of June 2026 - a clearly weaker pocket. Some sources cite standalone PAT growth as high as ~76% versus ~56% consolidated; these are not strictly comparable given different consolidation scopes, so the consolidated figure is the more reliable one. Q2 FY27 results are due 27 October 2026 - not yet out as of this report.",
    "performance_read": "This stock only began trading on 13 October 2025, so there is barely a year of price history (about 1.02 years) - there is no long-horizon evidence of how it behaves through a credit cycle, and none should be assumed. There have been no splits or demergers, so the price move below is a genuine market reassessment, not a corporate-action artifact. The stock is down 17.9% from its 6 August 2026 peak of \u20b9382.7, and currently sits at \u20b9314.25, closer to its 52-week low (\u20b9297.3) than its high. Despite that drawdown, since listing the stock's total return (-4.76%) has actually held up better than the Nifty (-9.27%) and peer Bajaj Finance (-7.09%) over the same window, and it has also beaten both over the last year.",
    "valuation_read": "At a P/E of roughly 24.8x (down from about 27.6x earlier) and P/B of about 2.9x, the market is pricing in a higher-return lending franchise than the numbers currently show - ROE is 10.6% and ROCE just 8.1%, both lagging more established NBFC peers. Why it might still carry a premium: Tata Group parentage, scale (AUM near \u20b92.9 lakh crore), and a new growth lever in gold loans. Whether it is actually cheap is a separate question - on current returns it isn't; at least one independent tracker (MarketsMojo) downgraded it to a 'Sell' valuation grade on 13 October 2026, citing the ROE/ROCE gap versus peers even after the multiple compression from its post-listing peak.",
    "growth_read": "Growth is volume-led: AUM is up 22% YoY overall and 28% YoY excluding motor finance, i.e., the loan book itself is expanding. However, the profit growth (56% YoY) outran the volume growth, largely because credit costs fell from 1.6% to 1.0% - a cost/provisioning tailwind layered on top of volume growth, not purely organic margin expansion. That tailwind is less durable than straightforward volume growth and bears watching in coming quarters.",
    "health_read": "As an NBFC, the relevant health question is asset quality and capital buffers rather than cash conversion. Capital adequacy is comfortable - CRAR of 18.5% against a 15% regulatory minimum, with Tier I at 15.6%. Consolidated debt-to-equity of about 5.3x is high in absolute terms but is the normal funding model for a lender of this scale, not by itself a red flag given the capital buffer. The concentrated risk sits in the motor finance segment specifically (gross stage-3 7.9%, net stage-3 5.1%), making this more a business/credit-quality issue in one segment than a company-wide financial risk.",
    "ownership_read": "Tata Sons and affiliated Tata entities together hold 85.41% of the company, unchanged across recent quarters, with no promoter pledging reported. That leaves a small free float - FIIs about 5.18%, DIIs about 3.56%, and public roughly 5.19%. There is no well-known individual marquee investor visible on the shareholding register; this is a promoter-dominated Tata Group holding rather than one with a notable outside individual stakeholder.",
    "strengths": [
      "AUM grew 22% YoY to about \u20b92.9 lakh crore in Q1 FY27 (28% YoY excluding motor finance)",
      "Credit cost improved to 1.0% from 1.6% a year earlier, helping PAT rise 56% YoY to \u20b91,547 crore",
      "Capital adequacy comfortable at 18.5% CRAR versus a 15% regulatory minimum",
      "Zero promoter pledging with Tata Sons and group entities holding 85.41%, signalling continued commitment"
    ],
    "watchpoints": [
      "Motor finance remains the weak link: gross stage-3 assets at 7.9% and net stage-3 at 5.1% as of June 2026",
      "Valuation looks rich relative to current returns - P/E ~25x, P/B ~2.9x against ROE 10.6% and ROCE 8.1%; one tracker downgraded it to 'Sell' on valuation on 13 October 2026",
      "Stock is 17.9% below its August 2026 peak and dividend yield is negligible at 0.18%",
      "Gold loan entry via the Yogloans acquisition is still pending RBI approval, adding integration/execution risk",
      "Only about one year of trading history exists; there is no track record through a full credit cycle yet"
    ],
    "one_thing": "The Q2 FY27 results due 27 October 2026 - specifically whether AUM growth and the lower credit-cost trend from Q1 continue, and whether motor finance stage-3 stress stabilises or worsens.",
    "data_confidence": "Moderate",
    "data_notes": "Q1 FY27 (Apr-Jun 2026) consolidated PAT (\u20b91,547 crore, +56% YoY), AUM (\u20b92.9 lakh crore, +22% YoY), credit cost and GNPA figures were cross-checked across BusinessToday, FreePressJournal, ScanX and Investing.com and are consistent. A standalone PAT growth figure (~76%) appeared in one source and was set aside as not comparable to the consolidated number. Shareholding (promoter 85.41%, no pledge) is sourced from post-listing exchange filings via Choice/StockSena. P/E, P/B, ROE and ROCE were sourced from MarketsMojo/Eulerpool as of early-to-mid October 2026 and were not independently cross-verified against Screener - treat as approximate. Q2 FY27 (quarter ended September 2026) results had not been released as of this report; the figures above are the last available (Q1 FY27)."
